DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     vveellcchheecckk proves a set of drawings made with vveelllluumm before
     any paper moves.  It writes one finding per line on standard
     output as _f_i_l_e:  _m_e_s_s_a_g_e, and its EXIT STATUS is the finding
     count (capped at 254), so mmaakkee(1) gates on it exactly the
     way it gates on cccc  -WWeerrrroorr:

                  check: ; velcheck amp?.d
                  print: check ; velplot amp?.d | lpr

     A clean run says so on standard error and exits 0.  The file
     name - is standard input.

DDRRAAWWIINNGG FFIINNDDIINNGGSS
              duplicate designators - across the whole SET, naming the
              other sheet
              a designated part with no value
              dangling wire ends: an endpoint touching no pin, no other
              wire and no net-name marker
              symbol codes no loaded library defines - the lines the
              loader would silently DROP, caught by re-reading the file
              sheets whose UU units disagree with the set's
              single-pin nets, and unconnected pins

     Then the ERC, between TYPED pins (see ssyymmeeddiitt): two outputs
     on one net conflict, an output must not drive a power rail,
     and a net whose typed pins are all inputs is undriven.  The
     rules fire only where the library types its pins, so an old
     library produces silence, not noise, and a half-typed one
     checks exactly as far as it is typed.

     The CONDUCTOR findings - dangling ends, nets, pins, ERC -
     apply only to a sheet that contains WIRES.  A diagram sheet
     (flowchart, floor plan, structure chart) uses stencils whose
     pins are attachment points, not terminals, and flagging
     those would be noise rather than findings.

LLIIBBRRAARRYY FFIINNDDIINNGGSS
     -ssyymm proves the stencil LIBRARIES the same way, and it mat-
     ters more: a wrong stencil is wrong in EVERY drawing.  It
     reads only the libraries it is given - the standard set is
     not loaded underneath - so ``defined in both'' means these
     files.

              a code defined twice, or defined in two of the libraries given
              a symbol with no geometry
              a designator prefix with no pins
              a pin off a whole grid unit, or a pin name that repeats


